For continuous variables every definition and proof in this section carries over with a \emph{joint density} $f(x,y)$, for which $P(a<X<b\text{ and }c<Y<d)=\int_a^b\int_c^d f(x,y)\,\dd y\,\dd x$: sums over $j$ and $k$ become integrals over $x$ and $y$, the single-variable density is $f_X(x)=\int f(x,y)\,\dd y$, and independence means $f(x,y)=f_X(x)f_Y(y)$.
